      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "Sucrose (C"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "12"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "22"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "11"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") breaks down into glucose (C"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "12"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") and fructose (C"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "12"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": "O"
                  },
                  {
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "text": ") in acidic solution. (Glucose and fructose have the same chemical formula but different structures.)"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "lcstnuzlqibup6p",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "31rzgum2c6eburr",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "dw1hcctcettyo4l",
                "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
                "src": "{\\mathrm C}_{12}{\\mathrm H}_{22}{\\mathrm O}_{11}\\left(aq\\right)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm H}_2\\mathrm O\\left(l\\right)\\;\\;\\xrightarrow {\\left[\\mathrm H^+\\right]}\\;\\;{\\mathrm C}_6{\\mathrm H}_{12}{\\mathrm O}_6\\left(aq\\right)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm C}_6{\\mathrm H}_{12}{\\mathrm O}_6 \\left(aq\\right)",
                "subtype": "latex",
                "type": "formula"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "The animation below illustrates the changes occurring on both the molecular and macroscopic levels while this reaction takes place at specific temperature and pH conditions. On the left is a visualization of sucrose molecules forming glucose and fructose molecules. On the right is a graph of the concentration changes for all three species. The actual experiment takes place over 12 hours; the animation was sped up to show this process in about 12 seconds. Watch the video and then answer the questions that follow."
                  }
                ],
                "id": "zqzj8pemqaqjs7d",
                "type": "p"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "id": "ok4l8fv2pr59kkg",
                    "text": " ",
                    "type": "text"
